<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kuba Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1625 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Nsheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bushong-led kingdom of the Kasai, famous for its royal portrait statues, raffia velvet textiles and long oral history of kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bushong tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shyaam-a-mbul-a-ngoong">Shyaam a Mbul a Ngoong</a> with founding the kingdom around 1625, uniting chiefdoms between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasai">Kasai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankuru-river">Sankuru</a> rivers. He is remembered as a culture hero who brought new crops, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, from the west, together with new political institutions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king (nyimi) ruled from the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsheng">Nsheng</a> with an elaborate council of titled officials, and the court sponsored an exceptional art tradition: wooden ndop statues commemorating each king, carved cups, masks and embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raffia-cloth">raffia cloth</a>. Agriculture and trade in raffia and camwood supported dense villages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Kuba</a> oral history was studied in detail by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-vansina">Jan Vansina</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zappo-zap">Zappo Zap</a> raiders working for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsheng">Nsheng</a>, and Belgian administration followed, though the kingship continued under colonial rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Kuba</a> remain known worldwide for their art, and the nyimi is still a traditional ruler in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kuba-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kuba Kingdom" width="320" height="394"><figcaption>Image: Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunda_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1625 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nsheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shyaam a Mbul a Ngoong, Kot aMbweeky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Kuba religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bushong</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Democratic Republic of the Congo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kuba, Bushongo</dd></dl></aside></div>

